# Repository connection delivery evidence

Demonstrated application and frontend: `9474a8121c3aadb9cacd236f005a77b54a0eafd1`.
Recorded on 2026-09-15, Asia/Hong_Kong. The source tree stayed unchanged throughout both isolated runs.

The stack used `stageAcceptanceRuntime` and `docker compose --env-file <disposable-env> -p poketto-delivery-final -f acceptance/compose.yaml up --build -d`: real Spring, PostgreSQL 17.11, production Next.js and Caddy. The managed-provider mock was disabled. Browser: Codex in-app browser, default viewport, screenshots are original unmodified JPEG bytes and were visually inspected. Synthetic accounts and display names contain no production data.

## Observations

- [Connection retry controls](connection-retry.jpg): a real browser submitted synthetic invalid credentials. The local proxy resolved GitHub to a non-public address; the application refused it. The form cleared the token and retained the request with retry, result query and replacement controls. Querying the result kept the same failure state.
- A fresh fixture used public GitHub addresses resolved over HTTPS through container-local host entries. TLS verification and private-address rejection stayed enabled. Real authenticated HTTP submitted an invalid token for an existing empty private repository, observed `PERMISSION_DENIED`, then supplied an existing valid credential to the same request. The same workspace reached `READY`.
- Result query and idempotent replay returned that workspace. A different request for the same repository returned `DUPLICATE`. Independent PostgreSQL readback found one encrypted binding, public delivery disabled, no failed workspace or membership, and no retained credential on the ready attempt. Independent GitHub branch listings remained empty before and after. No Git ref or remote file was changed.
- [Private website default](connection-private-default.jpg): after signing in to the second fixture, the browser selected the newly connected space and showed its website as disabled. The browser did not enable it or write repository content.

[checks.json](checks.json) records source identity, response stages, database assertions and image hashes. Both fixture stacks, volumes and networks were removed, including their temporary encrypted credential storage. Production was not modified.

## Scope

Provider metadata and Git upload-pack/receive-pack advertisement used real GitHub services. Content reading remained routed to the synthetic Git fixture. This verifies connection and binding lifecycle, not remote content writes. CNB interoperability was not exercised. The screenshots show two separate fixture runs; they are not a continuous recording of credential replacement. Public provider coordinates, tokens and private repository identity are intentionally absent from this artifact branch.

This branch contains review evidence only and must not be merged into product history.
